## Error handling

Every provider throws the **same** normalised `PostboiError` on failure: HTTP errors,
provider error envelopes, timeouts, and network failures all funnel through it. Error
handling is identical no matter which provider you use, and the original provider payload is
kept on `.raw`.

```typescript
try {
	await mail.send({ to: 'bad@email', body: 'test' })
} catch (error) {
	if (mail.is_error(error)) {
		// error is a PostboiError
		console.error(error.provider) // e.g. "resend"
		console.error(error.status) // HTTP status, when applicable
		console.error(error.code) // provider-specific code, when available
		console.error(error.message) // normalised message
		console.error(error.raw) // the original provider payload
		console.error(error.outcome_unknown) // true when it may have been sent anyway
		console.error(error.reason) // why it was refused: "auth", "recipient", … (see below)
	} else {
		console.error(error)
	}
}
```

### Why it was refused

`reason` says why a provider refused, the same words whichever provider it was: `auth`,
`rate_limited`, `sender`, `account`, `recipient`, `compliance` or `request`. It comes from
the provider's own error code where postboi knows it (Twilio's, AWS's), and from the HTTP
status otherwise. It is never set on an unknown outcome, since nothing was refused.

The first four are about the provider rather than the message, so another provider might
accept the same message; that is what an [SMS or WhatsApp provider chain](/sms#failing-over)
fails over on, and `fails_over(error)` is the same test. A chain that ran out of providers
throws a `FailoverError` (still a `PostboiError`) whose `errors` lists every answer.

### Common codes

Beyond provider-specific codes passed through on `.code`, postboi itself uses a few:

| Code | Meaning |
| ---- | ------- |
| `skipped` / `spam` | The send was intentionally cancelled: a `before.send` hook or the [honeypot](/spam). |
| `provider_chain_not_supported` | Several providers named on a channel that sends through one (push, chat). |
| `no_provider` / `no_token` / `unknown_provider` / `missing_env` | Zero-config resolution failed. See [Quick start](/quick-start). In development a missing credential [logs instead of throwing](/providers#no-credential-yet). |
| `cancel_not_supported` | [`cancel()`](/scheduling#cancelling-a-scheduled-send) on a provider without a cancellation API. |
| `webhooks_not_supported` | [`receive()`](/webhooks) for a provider that emits no delivery events. |
| `invalid_payload` | A webhook body that doesn't parse. |

Webhook verification failures throw a `WebhookVerificationError` (a `PostboiError`
subclass) with codes `invalid_signature`, `missing_secret`, `stale_timestamp` or
`unsupported_runtime`. Return a 401 when you catch one.

## Retries

Every provider accepts `retries`, `retry_delay`, and `timeout`:

```typescript
// postboi.config.ts
import { config } from 'postboi'

export default config({
	timeout: 30000, // per-request timeout in ms (default 30000)
	retries: 2, // retries on 429; on 5xx and network errors only with an idempotency_key (default 0)
	retry_delay: 500 // base backoff in ms, doubles each attempt (default 500)
})
```

Nothing is retried until `retries` is above 0. Then what gets retried depends on what
the failure proves. The delay doubles each attempt
(exponential backoff), and a `Retry-After` from the provider is honoured up to a minute.

| Failure | Retried | `outcome_unknown` |
| ------- | ------- | ----------------- |
| `429` | Yes. The provider refused the request. | `false` |
| `5xx`, timeout, network error | Only when the send carries an `idempotency_key` the provider honours. | `true` |
| Any other `4xx` | Never. Sending it again gets the same answer. | `false` |

A `5xx` or a dropped connection doesn't mean the message didn't go. The provider may have
queued it before answering badly or not at all, so sending it again can deliver it twice.
That is why those retries wait for a key the provider deduplicates on, and why the error
says `outcome_unknown: true` when they run out: look the message up with the provider
before you send it again yourself.

Providers that honour a key: the Postboi provider, Resend, AhaSend, Lettermint, JetEmail,
Loops, Primitive, Klaviyo (`unique_id`), HubSpot (`sendId`) and Azure (a UUID, as its
`Operation-Id`). None of the SMS providers documents one for sending, Twilio included, so
a text is never retried after a `5xx`.

If a duplicate is better than a drop for you, `retry_unknown: true` retries those failures
without a key too.

> **Retries are off by default on purpose.** Set `retries` and pass an `idempotency_key`
> on sends to a provider that honours one.
